<body>

    <div class="layout-container">

        <!-- Header Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <h1 style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Simple Pendulum Lab</h1>
            <p style="color: var(--text-muted);">Investigate the relationship between mass, length, and the period of oscillation.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Interactive Simulation Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Live Sandbox</div>

            <div class="simulation-grid">
                <canvas id="pendulumCanvas"></canvas>

                <div class="controls">
                    <div class="control-group">
                        <label>
                            Mass (m)
                            <span id="val-mass">1.0 kg</span>
                        </label>
                        <input type="range" id="input-mass" min="0.1" max="5.0" step="0.1" value="1.0">
                    </div>

                    <div class="control-group">
                        <label>
                            Length (L)
                            <span id="val-length">1.0 m</span>
                        </label>
                        <input type="range" id="input-length" min="0.1" max="3.0" step="0.1" value="1.0">
                    </div>

                    <div class="control-group">
                        <label>
                            Gravity (g)
                            <span id="val-gravity">9.8 m/s²</span>
                        </label>
                        <input type="range" id="input-gravity" min="1.6" max="20.0" step="0.1" value="9.8">
                    </div>

                    <div style="margin-top: auto; display: flex; gap: 10px;">
                        <button class="btn" id="btn-toggle">Pause</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-outline" id="btn-reset">Reset</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="margin-top: 20px; padding: 15px; background: #f8fafc; border-radius: 8px;">
                <strong>Current Period (T): </strong> <span id="display-period" style="color: var(--primary); font-family: monospace; font-size: 1.2rem;">2.01 s</span>
                <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 5px;">
                    The time taken for one complete back-and-forth swing.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Quick Definition Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Quick Definition</div>
            <p>
                A <strong>simple pendulum</strong> consists of a point mass (bob) suspended from a fixed point by a massless, inextensible string. It is an idealized physical system used to demonstrate harmonic motion.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Formula Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Period Formula</div>

            <div class="formula-container">
                $$ T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} $$
            </div>

            <div class="formula-actions">
                <button class="btn btn-outline" style="padding: 8px 16px; font-size: 0.9rem;" onclick="showToast('Mass is not in the formula! The period depends on Length and Gravity only.')">Explain Formula</button>
                <button class="btn" style="padding: 8px 16px; font-size: 0.9rem;" onclick="scrollToSim()">Open Formula Lab</button>
            </div>

            <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Symbol</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                            <th>Role</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>T</strong></td>
                            <td>Period (seconds)</td>
                            <td><span class="badge badge-variable">Dependent</span></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>L</strong></td>
                            <td>Length of string (meters)</td>
                            <td><span class="badge badge-variable">Independent</span></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>g</strong></td>
                            <td>Acceleration due to gravity (m/s²)</td>
                            <td><span class="badge badge-variable">Independent</span></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>m</strong></td>
                            <td>Mass of bob (kg)</td>
                            <td><span class="badge badge-constant">Not Present</span></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Analysis Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Analysis: The Mass Paradox</div>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
                Intuitively, you might think a heavier object would swing faster or slower. However, the simulation and formula demonstrate that <strong>mass does not affect the period</strong> of a simple pendulum.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Why?</strong><br>
                While a heavier mass experiences a larger gravitational force ($F = mg$), it also has more inertia (resistance to acceleration, $F = ma$). These two effects cancel each other out perfectly, resulting in the same acceleration regardless of mass.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Key Concepts Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Key Concepts</div>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; display: grid; gap: 10px;">
                <li><strong>Restoring Force:</strong> The component of gravity acting tangential to the arc pulls the bob back to equilibrium.</li>
                <li><strong>Simple Harmonic Motion (SHM):</strong> For small angles (< 15°), the motion approximates SHM.</li>
                <li><strong>Amplitude Independence:</strong> For small angles, the period is independent of how far back you pull the pendulum.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Suggested Questions Card -->
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header">Suggested Questions</div>
            <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px;">
                <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px; cursor: pointer;">
                    <summary style="font-weight: 600;">What happens to the period if you double the length?</summary>
                    <p style="margin-top: 8px; color: var(--text-muted);">Since $T \propto \sqrt{L}$, doubling the length increases the period by $\sqrt{2} \approx 1.41$. The pendulum swings slower.</p>
                </details>
                <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px; cursor: pointer;">
                    <summary style="font-weight: 600;">Why would a pendulum clock run slow on the moon?</summary>
                    <p style="margin-top: 8px; color: var(--text-muted);">Gravity on the moon is about 1/6th of Earth's. Since $T \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{g}}$, lower gravity means a larger period (longer time to swing), making the clock run slow.</p>
                </details>
                <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px; cursor: pointer;">
                    <summary style="font-weight: 600;">Does mass matter in a REAL pendulum?</summary>
                    <p style="margin-top: 8px; color: var(--text-muted);">In a real physical pendulum (with a rod of significant mass), the distribution of mass matters, but the total mass still generally cancels out if air resistance is negligible.</p>
                </details>
            </div>
        </div>

